 <exercise xml:id="lincombs-practice-1">
  <statement>
    Determine whether the vector
    <me>
      \mathbf{b} =
      \begin{bmatrix} 4 \\ 1 \\ 2 \end{bmatrix}
    </me>
    is a linear combination of
    <me>
      \mathbf{v}_1 =
      \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
      \quad \text{and} \quad
      \mathbf{v}_2 =
      \begin{bmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}.
    </me>
    If it is, find scalars that express <m>\mathbf{b}</m> as such a combination.
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p> Try to solve the vector equation
    <m>a\mathbf{v}_1 + b\mathbf{v}_2 = \mathbf{b}</m>
    by comparing components. </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
    Yes, <m>\mathbf{b}</m> is a linear combination of <m>\mathbf{v}_1</m> and
    <m>\mathbf{v}_2</m>.
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p> We solve
    <me>
      a\begin{bmatrix}1\\0\\1 \end{bmatrix}
      +
      b\begin{bmatrix}2\\1\\0 \end{bmatrix}
      =
      \begin{bmatrix}4\\1\\2 \end{bmatrix}.
    </me>
    This gives the system
    <me>
      \begin{aligned}
        a + 2b \amp = 4 \\
        b \amp = 1 \\
        a \amp = 2.
      \end{aligned}
    </me>
     The equations are consistent, so
    <m>\mathbf{b} = 2\mathbf{v}_1 + 1\mathbf{v}_2</m>. 
  </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="sec2-2-core-idea">
  <statement>
    Consider the augmented matrix
    <me>
      \begin{bmatrix}
        1 \amp 2 \amp -1 \amp 3 \\
        0 \amp 1 \amp  2 \amp 1
      \end{bmatrix}.
    </me>
    <ol>
      <li>Write a system of linear equations represented by this matrix.</li>
      <li>Identify which variables are pivot variables and which are free.</li>
      <li>Explain why performing row operations on this matrix does not change
          the solution set of the system.</li>
    </ol>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p> Each row corresponds to one equation, and pivot variables correspond to
    leading entries.</p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p> The system has two pivot variables and one free variable, and row operations
    preserve the solution set.</p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p> The matrix represents the system
    <me>
      \begin{aligned}
        x + 2y - z \amp = 3 \\
        y + 2z \amp = 1.
      \end{aligned}
    </me>
    The leading entries occur in the <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> columns, so
    <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> are pivot variables, while <m>z</m> is free.
  </p>
  <p>
    Row operations correspond to rewriting equations using algebraic steps
    such as swapping equations, scaling them, or replacing one equation with
    a combination of others. These operations do not change the set of solutions,
    only the way the system is written.
  </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="sec2-3-core-1">
  <statement>
    Consider the matrix equation
    <me>
      \begin{bmatrix}
        1 \amp 2 \amp 1 \\
        0 \amp 1 \amp -1 \\
        1 \amp 3 \amp 0
      \end{bmatrix}
      \begin{bmatrix}
        x_1 \\ x_2 \\ x_3
      \end{bmatrix}
      =
      \begin{bmatrix}
        4 \\ 0 \\ 4
      \end{bmatrix}.
    </me>
    <ol>
      <li>Rewrite this equation as a vector equation involving the columns of the matrix.</li>
      <li>Determine whether the equation has a solution.</li>
    </ol>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Think of the right-hand side as a linear combination of the columns of the
      matrix. Solve for the coefficients.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      Yes, the equation has a solution; the right-hand side is a linear combination
      of the columns.
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      Writing the equation in vector form gives
    </p>
    <me>
      x_1
      \begin{bmatrix}1\\0\\1\end{bmatrix}
      +
      x_2
      \begin{bmatrix}2\\1\\3\end{bmatrix}
      +
      x_3
      \begin{bmatrix}1\\-1\\0\end{bmatrix}
      =
      \begin{bmatrix}4\\0\\4\end{bmatrix}.
    </me>
    <p>
      Solving the system yields <m>x_1 = 1</m>, <m>x_2 = 1</m>, and <m>x_3 = 1</m>.
      Therefore, the vector on the right lies in the span of the columns, and the
      matrix equation is consistent.
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="sec2-3-core-2">
  <statement>
    Let
    <me>
      A =
      \begin{bmatrix}
        1 \amp 0 \amp 2 \\
        2 \amp 1 \amp 4 \\
        0 \amp 1 \amp 1
      \end{bmatrix}
    </me>
    and
    <me>
      \mathbf{b} =
      \begin{bmatrix}
        3 \\ 7 \\ 2
      \end{bmatrix}.
    </me>
    Decide whether the equation <m>A\mathbf{x} = \mathbf{b}</m> has a solution by
    interpreting it as a vector equation.
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Ask whether <m>\mathbf{b}</m> can be written as a linear combination of the
      columns of <m>A</m>.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      Yes. The equation does have a solution, <m>
      \mathbf{x} =
      \begin{bmatrix}
        1 \\ 1 \\ 1
      \end{bmatrix}
    </m> 
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      The corresponding vector equation is
    </p>
    <me>
      x_1
      \begin{bmatrix}1\\2\\0\end{bmatrix}
      +
      x_2
      \begin{bmatrix}0\\1\\1\end{bmatrix}
      +
      x_3
      \begin{bmatrix}2\\4\\1\end{bmatrix}
      =
      \begin{bmatrix}3\\7\\2\end{bmatrix}.
    </me>
    <p>
      Solving this system leads to a solution <m>
      \mathbf{x} =
      \begin{bmatrix}
        1 \\ 1 \\ 1
      \end{bmatrix}
    </m>, showing that
      <m>\mathbf{b}</m> is in the span of the columns of <m>A</m>.
      Therefore, the matrix equation has a solution.
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="sec2-3-span-core">
  <statement>
    Let
    <me>
      \mathbf{v}_1 =
      \begin{bmatrix}
        1 \\ 0 \\ 1
      \end{bmatrix},
      \quad
      \mathbf{v}_2 =
      \begin{bmatrix}
        2 \\ 1 \\ 3
      \end{bmatrix}.
    </me>
    <ol>
      <li>Describe the set <m>\text{span}\{\mathbf{v}_1, \mathbf{v}_2\}</m> geometrically.</li>
      <li>Determine whether the vector
        <me>
          \mathbf{w} =
          \begin{bmatrix}
            3 \\ 1 \\ 4
          \end{bmatrix}
        </me>
        lies in this span.</li>
    </ol>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      The span consists of all linear combinations of the given vectors.
      To test membership, try to solve
      <m>a\mathbf{v}_1 + b\mathbf{v}_2 = \mathbf{w}</m>.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      The span is a plane through the origin, and <m>\mathbf{w}</m> lies in this plane.
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      Since <m>\mathbf{v}_1</m> and <m>\mathbf{v}_2</m> are not scalar multiples,
      their span is a plane through the origin in <m>\mathbb{R}^3</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      To see whether <m>\mathbf{w}</m> lies in the span, we solve
    </p>
    <me>
      a
      \begin{bmatrix}
        1 \\ 0 \\ 1
      \end{bmatrix}
      +
      b
      \begin{bmatrix}
        2 \\ 1 \\ 3
      \end{bmatrix}
      =
      \begin{bmatrix}
        3 \\ 1 \\ 4
      \end{bmatrix}.
    </me>
    <p>
      This leads to the system
      <me>
        \begin{aligned}
          a + 2b \amp = 3 \\
          b \amp = 1 \\
          a + 3b \amp = 4.
        \end{aligned}
      </me>
      The system is consistent with <m>a = 1</m> and <m>b = 1</m>.
      Therefore, <m>\mathbf{w}</m> lies in the span.
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="sec2-4-linind-1">
  <statement>
    Determine whether the vectors
    <me>
      \mathbf{v}_1 =
      \begin{bmatrix}
        1 \\ 2 \\ 1
      \end{bmatrix},
      \quad
      \mathbf{v}_2 =
      \begin{bmatrix}
        2 \\ 4 \\ 2
      \end{bmatrix},
      \quad
      \mathbf{v}_3 =
      \begin{bmatrix}
        0 \\ 1 \\ -1
      \end{bmatrix}
    </me>
    are linearly independent.
    Justify your answer using the definition of linear independence.
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Ask whether a nontrivial linear combination of the vectors can equal
      the zero vector.  Look for obvious scalar multiples.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      The vectors are linearly dependent.
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      The vector <m>\mathbf{v}_2</m> is twice <m>\mathbf{v}_1</m>.
      Therefore,
      <m>\mathbf{v}_2 - 2\mathbf{v}_1 = \mathbf{0}</m>
      is a nontrivial linear combination that equals the zero vector.
    </p>
    <p>
      Since not all coefficients are zero, the set of vectors is linearly
      dependent by definition.
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="sec2-4-linind-2">
  <statement>
    Suppose three vectors in <m>\mathbb{R}^3</m> all lie in the same plane
    through the origin.
    <ol>
      <li>Can these three vectors be linearly independent?  Explain.</li>
      <li>Explain how your answer follows from the definition of linear independence.</li>
    </ol>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Think about how many directions are needed to describe all vectors in a plane.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      No, the three vectors cannot be linearly independent.
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      A plane through the origin in <m>\mathbb{R}^3</m> can be described as the
      span of two linearly independent vectors.
      Any third vector in the same plane can be written as a linear combination
      of those two.
    </p>
    <p>
      Therefore, one of the three vectors can be expressed as a linear combination
      of the others, which means the set is linearly dependent by definition.
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="sec2-4-linind-rref">
  <statement>
    Determine whether the vectors
    <me>
      \mathbf{v}_1 =
      \begin{bmatrix}
        1 \\ 2 \\ 1
      \end{bmatrix},
      \quad
      \mathbf{v}_2 =
      \begin{bmatrix}
        2 \\ 3 \\ 1
      \end{bmatrix},
      \quad
      \mathbf{v}_3 =
      \begin{bmatrix}
        1 \\ 1 \\ 0
      \end{bmatrix}
    </me>
    are linearly independent.
    You must justify your answer using reduced row echelon form.
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Form a matrix whose columns are the given vectors and compute its
      reduced row echelon form.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      The vectors are linearly dependent.
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      Form the matrix with the vectors as columns:
    </p>
    <me>
      \begin{bmatrix}
        1 \amp 2 \amp 1 \\
        2 \amp 3 \amp 1 \\
        1 \amp 1 \amp 0
      \end{bmatrix}.
    </me>
    <p>
      Row‑reducing this matrix yields the reduced row echelon form
    </p>
    <me>
      \begin{bmatrix}
        1 \amp 2 \amp 1 \\
        2 \amp 3 \amp 1 \\
        1 \amp 1 \amp 0
      \end{bmatrix} \sim 
      \begin{bmatrix}
        1 \amp 2 \amp 1 \\
        0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\
        0 \amp -1 \amp -1
      \end{bmatrix} \sim
      \begin{bmatrix}
        1 \amp 0 \amp -1 \\
        0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\
        0 \amp 0 \amp 0
      \end{bmatrix}.
    </me>
    <p>
      Since there is a free variable, and the system is consistent, there are infinitely many solutions.  
      So, <m>c_1\mathbf{v}_1 + c_2\mathbf{v}_2 + c_3\mathbf{v}_3 = \mathbf{0}</m> when <m>c_1=1</m>, <m>c_2=-1</m>, and <m>c_3=1</m> (for example). 
      Therefore, the vectors are linearly dependent.
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="sec2-4-param-linind">
  <statement>
    For what value(s) of <m>k</m> are the vectors
    <me>
      \mathbf{v}_1 =
      \begin{bmatrix}
        1 \\ k \\ 2
      \end{bmatrix},
      \quad
      \mathbf{v}_2 =
      \begin{bmatrix}
        2 \\ 3 \\ 4
      \end{bmatrix},
      \quad
      \mathbf{v}_3 =
      \begin{bmatrix}
        1 \\ 1 \\ k
      \end{bmatrix}
    </me>
    linearly independent?
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Form a matrix using the vectors as columns and determine when a pivot
      fails to appear.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      The vectors are linearly independent for all values of <m>k</m> except
      <m>k = 2</m>.
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      Place the vectors as columns of a matrix:
    </p>
    <me>
      \begin{bmatrix}
        1 \amp 2 \amp 1 \\
        k \amp 3 \amp 1 \\
        2 \amp 4 \amp k
      \end{bmatrix}.
    </me>
    <p>
      Row‑reducing yields a pivot in every column unless
      <m>k - 2 = 0</m>.
      When <m>k = 2</m>, one column becomes a linear combination of the others.
    </p>
    <p>
      Therefore, the vectors are linearly independent for all
      <m>k \neq 2</m>, and linearly dependent when <m>k = 2</m>.
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="sec2-5-matrix-trans-columns">
  <statement>
    Let <m>T : \mathbb{R}^2 \to \mathbb{R}^2</m> be the linear transformation defined by
    <me>
      T(\mathbf{x}) =
      \begin{bmatrix}
        2 \amp 1 \\
        -1 \amp 1
      \end{bmatrix}
      \mathbf{x}.
    </me>
    <ol>
      <li>Compute <m>T(\mathbf{e}_1)</m> and <m>T(\mathbf{e}_2)</m>, where
      <m>\mathbf{e}_1 = \begin{bmatrix}1\\0\end{bmatrix}</m> and
      <m>\mathbf{e}_2 = \begin{bmatrix}0\\1\end{bmatrix}</m>.</li>
      <li>Explain how these images determine the transformation <m>T</m>.</li>
    </ol>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Multiply the matrix by each standard basis vector.
      Recall that every vector in <m>\mathbb{R}^2</m> can be written as a linear
      combination of <m>\mathbf{e}_1</m> and <m>\mathbf{e}_2</m>.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      <m>T(\mathbf{e}_1) = \begin{bmatrix}2\\-1\end{bmatrix}</m> and
      <m>T(\mathbf{e}_2) = \begin{bmatrix}1\\1\end{bmatrix}</m>.
      These vectors determine the entire transformation.
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      We compute
      <me>
        T(\mathbf{e}_1) =
        \begin{bmatrix}
          2 \amp 1 \\
          -1 \amp 1
        \end{bmatrix}
        \begin{bmatrix}1\\0\end{bmatrix}
        =
        \begin{bmatrix}2\\-1\end{bmatrix},
        \quad
        T(\mathbf{e}_2) =
        \begin{bmatrix}
          2 \amp 1 \\
          -1 \amp 1
        \end{bmatrix}
        \begin{bmatrix}0\\1\end{bmatrix}
        =
        \begin{bmatrix}1\\1\end{bmatrix}.
      </me>
    </p>
    <p>
      Every vector <m>\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{R}^2</m> can be written as
      <m>\mathbf{x} = a\mathbf{e}_1 + b\mathbf{e}_2</m>.
      Linearity implies
      <m>T(\mathbf{x}) = aT(\mathbf{e}_1) + bT(\mathbf{e}_2)</m>.
      Therefore, knowing the images of the standard basis vectors completely
      determines the transformation.
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="sec2-5-matrix-trans-columns-2">
  <statement>
    Let <m>T : \mathbb{R}^2 \to \mathbb{R}^2</m> be the linear transformation defined by
    <me>
      T(\mathbf{x}) =
      \begin{bmatrix}
        1 \amp 3 \\
        0 \amp 1
      \end{bmatrix}
      \mathbf{x}.
    </me>
    <ol>
      <li>Compute <m>T(\mathbf{e}_1)</m> and <m>T(\mathbf{e}_2)</m>, where
      <m>\mathbf{e}_1 = \begin{bmatrix}1\\0\end{bmatrix}</m> and
      <m>\mathbf{e}_2 = \begin{bmatrix}0\\1\end{bmatrix}</m>.</li>
      <li>Describe the geometric effect of <m>T</m> on vectors in <m>\mathbb{R}^2</m>.</li>
      <li>Describe the image of the unit square under <m>T</m>.</li>
    </ol>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      Compute the images of the standard basis vectors. The image of the unit
      square is determined by the images of these vectors.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      <m>T(\mathbf{e}_1) = \begin{bmatrix}1\\0\end{bmatrix}</m> and
      <m>T(\mathbf{e}_2) = \begin{bmatrix}3\\1\end{bmatrix}</m>.
      The transformation is a shear, and the unit square maps to a parallelogram.
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      We compute
      <me>
        T(\mathbf{e}_1) =
        \begin{bmatrix}
          1 \amp 3 \\
          0 \amp 1
        \end{bmatrix}
        \begin{bmatrix}1\\0\end{bmatrix}
        =
        \begin{bmatrix}1\\0\end{bmatrix},
        \quad
        T(\mathbf{e}_2) =
        \begin{bmatrix}
          1 \amp 3 \\
          0 \amp 1
        \end{bmatrix}
        \begin{bmatrix}0\\1\end{bmatrix}
        =
        \begin{bmatrix}3\\1\end{bmatrix}.
      </me>
    </p>
    <p>
      The transformation leaves horizontal vectors unchanged while shifting
      vertical vectors to the right, producing a shear.
      Since the unit square is spanned by <m>\mathbf{e}_1</m> and <m>\mathbf{e}_2</m>,
      its image is the parallelogram spanned by <m>T(\mathbf{e}_1)</m> and
      <m>T(\mathbf{e}_2)</m>.
    </p>

    <image width="50%">
    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
             label="parallelogram-image-trans-01-31">
<diagram dimensions="(300, 300)" margins="5">
  <coordinates bbox="(-1,-1,4,4)">
    <grid-axes decorations="no"/>
    <vector v="(3,1)" stroke="blue" alignment="nw"> <m>T(\mathbf{e}_2)</m> </vector>
    <vector v="(1,0)" tail="(3,1)" stroke="blue"/>
    <vector v="(3,1)" tail="(1,0)" stroke="blue"/>
    <vector v="(1,0)" stroke="blue" alignment="s"> <m>T(\mathbf{e}_1)</m> </vector>
    <polygon points="(0,0),(3,1),(4,1),(1,0)" fill="blue" opacity="0.4"/>
  </coordinates>
</diagram>
    </prefigure>
  </image>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="sec2-6-geometry-matrix">
  <statement>
    The figure below shows the image of the unit square in <m>\mathbb{R}^2</m>
    under a linear transformation <m>T</m>.
    <image xml:id="myfirstgeomtrans" width="60%"> 
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
             label="parallelogram-image-trans-11-n12">
      <diagram dimensions="(300, 300)" margins="5">
        <coordinates bbox="(-1,-1,3,3)">
          <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
          <vector v="(-1,2)" stroke="blue" alignment="nw">
            <m>T(\mathbf{e}_2)</m>
          </vector>
          <vector v="(1,1)" stroke="red" alignment="e">
            <m>T(\mathbf{e}_1)</m>
          </vector>
          <polygon points="(0,0),(1,1),(0,3),(-1,2)" fill="blue" opacity="0.25"/>
        </coordinates>
      </diagram>
    </prefigure>
  </image>
    <ol>
      <li>Write the matrix of <m>T</m>.</li>
      <li>Explain how the diagram determines this matrix.</li>
    </ol>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      The columns of the matrix of a linear transformation are the images of the
      standard basis vectors <m>\mathbf{e}_1</m> and <m>\mathbf{e}_2</m>.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      The matrix of <m>T</m> is
      <m>
        \begin{bmatrix}
          1 \amp -1 \\
          1 \amp 2
        \end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      From the diagram, we see that
      <m>T(\mathbf{e}_1) = \begin{bmatrix}1\\1\end{bmatrix}</m>
      and
      <m>T(\mathbf{e}_2) = \begin{bmatrix}-1\\2\end{bmatrix}</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      For a linear transformation, the matrix is formed by placing
      <m>T(\mathbf{e}_1)</m> and <m>T(\mathbf{e}_2)</m> as the first and second
      columns, respectively.
      Therefore,
    </p>
    <me>
      \begin{bmatrix}
        1 \amp -1 \\
        1 \amp 2
      \end{bmatrix}
    </me>
    <p>
      is the matrix of <m>T</m>.
    </p>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="sec2-6-projection-guided">
  <statement>
    Let <m>T : \mathbb{R}^2 \to \mathbb{R}^2</m> be the linear transformation
    that projects every vector onto the line <m>y = x</m>.
    <ol>
      <li>Any vector on the line <m>y=x</m> can be written in the form
        <m>t\begin{bmatrix}1\\1\end{bmatrix}</m>.
        Use this to explain why
        <m>T(\mathbf{e}_1)</m> must have this form.</li>
      <li>Find the value of <m>t</m> such that the vector
        <m>\mathbf{e}_1 - t\begin{bmatrix}1\\1\end{bmatrix}</m>
        is perpendicular to the line <m>y=x</m>.</li>
      <li>Use your result to compute <m>T(\mathbf{e}_1)</m> and
        <m>T(\mathbf{e}_2)</m>, then write the matrix of <m>T</m>.</li>
    </ol>
  </statement>

  <hint>
    <p>
      A vector is perpendicular to the line <m>y=x</m> if it is perpendicular to
      <m>\begin{bmatrix}1\\1\end{bmatrix}</m>.  Use the dot product.
    </p>
  </hint>

  <answer>
    <p>
      <m>T(\mathbf{e}_1) = T(\mathbf{e}_2) = \tfrac12\begin{bmatrix}1\\1\end{bmatrix}</m>,
      and the matrix of <m>T</m> is
      <m>
        \begin{bmatrix}
          \tfrac12 \amp \tfrac12 \\
          \tfrac12 \amp \tfrac12
        \end{bmatrix}.
      </m>
    </p>
  </answer>

  <solution>
    <p>
      Since the image of any vector under the projection lies on the line
      <m>y=x</m>, we know that
      <m>T(\mathbf{e}_1) = t\begin{bmatrix}1\\1\end{bmatrix}</m>
      for some scalar <m>t</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The vector from <m>T(\mathbf{e}_1)</m> to <m>\mathbf{e}_1</m> must be
      perpendicular to the line <m>y=x</m>.  Therefore,
    </p>
    <me>
      \left(
        \begin{bmatrix}1\\0\end{bmatrix}
        -
        t\begin{bmatrix}1\\1\end{bmatrix}
      \right)
      \cdot
      \begin{bmatrix}1\\1\end{bmatrix}
      = 0.
    </me>
    <p>
      Computing the dot product gives
      <m>1 - 2t = 0</m>, so <m>t = \tfrac12</m>.
      Thus,
      <m>T(\mathbf{e}_1) = \tfrac12\begin{bmatrix}1\\1\end{bmatrix}</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      By symmetry, <m>\mathbf{e}_2</m> projects to the same point on the line,
      so
      <m>T(\mathbf{e}_2) = \tfrac12\begin{bmatrix}1\\1\end{bmatrix}</m>.
      Placing these vectors as columns gives the matrix
    </p>
    <me>
      \begin{bmatrix}
        \tfrac12 \amp \tfrac12 \\
        \tfrac12 \amp \tfrac12
      \end{bmatrix}.
    </me>
  </solution>
</exercise>

<!-- Based on OPL problem at Library/WHFreeman/Holt_linear_algebra/Chaps_1-4/2.3.47.pg -->
    <exercise xml:id="ex-ww-li1" label="ex-ww-li1">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>\{\mathbf{v}_1,\mathbf{v}_2,\mathbf{v}_3,\mathbf{v}_4\}</m> be a linearly independent set of vectors.
          Select the best statement.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="no">
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>\{\mathbf{v}_1,\mathbf{v}_2,\mathbf{v}_3\}</m> is never a linearly independent set of vectors.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="no">
          <statement>
            <p>
              The independence of the set <m>\{\vvec_1,\vvec_2,\vvec_3\}</m> depends on the vectors chosen.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>\{\vvec_1,\vvec_2,\vvec_3\}</m> is always a linearly independent set of vectors.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>

    <!-- Based on the OPL problem at Library/WHFreeman/Holt_linear_algebra/Chaps_1-4/2.3.49.pg -->
    <exercise xml:id="ex-ww-li2" label="ex-ww-li2">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>\vvec_4</m> be a linear combination of <m>\{\vvec_1,\vvec_2,\vvec_3\}</m>.
          Select the best statement.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>\{\vvec_1,\vvec_2,\vvec_3,\vvec_4\}</m> is never a linearly independent set of vectors.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="no">
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>\{\vvec_1,\vvec_2,\vvec_3,\vvec_4\}</m> is always a linearly independent set of vectors.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="no">
          <statement>
            <p>
              We can't conclude whether or not <m>\{\vvec_1,\vvec_2,\vvec_3,\vvec_4\}</m> is a linearly independent set of vectors.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="no">
          <statement>
            <p>
              The set <m>\{\vvec_1,\vvec_2,\vvec_3\}</m> must be a linearly independent set of vectors.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="no">
          <statement>
            <p>
              The set <m>\{\vvec_1,\vvec_2,\vvec_3\}</m> cannot be a linearly independent set of vectors.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>

    <!-- Based on the OPL problem at Library/WHFreeman/Holt_linear_algebra/Chaps_1-4/2.3.52.pg -->
    <exercise xml:id="ex-ww-li3" label="ex-ww-li3">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Assume <m>\vvec_4</m> is not a linear combination of the vectors <m>\vvec_1,\vvec_2,\vvec_3</m>.
          Select the best statement.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="no">
          <statement>
            <p>
              The set <m>\{\vvec_1,\vvec_2,\vvec_3,\vvec_4\}</m> is always linearly independent.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="no">
          <statement>
            <p>
              The set <m>\{\vvec_1,\vvec_2,\vvec_3,\vvec_4\}</m> is never linearly independent.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>
              The set <m>\{\vvec_1,\vvec_2,\vvec_3,\vvec_4\}</m> is linearly independent provided that <m>\{\vvec_1,\vvec_2,\vvec_3\}</m> is linearly independent.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="ex-mc-transf-matrix1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        What is the size of the matrix <m>A</m> used for the matrix transformation <m>T:\R^n\to \R^m</m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>m\times n</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct! We need to be able to multiply on the right by an <m>n\times 1</m>
            column vector, and get an <m>m\times 1</m> column vector as output.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="no">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>n\times m</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            The domain of <m>T</m> is <m>\R^n</m>,
            and the product <m>A\xvec</m> is only defined if the number of columns (<m>m</m>)
            is equal to the dimension of the domain.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="no">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>m\times m</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            The domain of <m>T</m> is <m>\R^n</m>,
            and the product <m>A\xvec</m> is only defined if the number of columns (<m>m</m>)
            is equal to the dimension of the domain.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="no">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>n\times n</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Although the product <m>A\xvec</m> would be defined in this case,
            the result would be a vector in <m>\R^n</m>, and we want a vector in <m>\R^m</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <exercise label="ex-rs-mc-span" xml:id="ex-rs-mc-span">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>S = \{\vvec_1,\ldots, \vvec_k\}</m> be a set of vectors.
        Which of the following statements are equivalent to the statement,
        <q>The vector <m>\wvec</m> belongs to the span of <m>S</m>.</q>?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="no">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\wvec=\vvec_1+\cdots +\vvec_k</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            This is a tricky one: the statement implies that <m>\wvec</m> the span of <m>S</m>,
            but it is not equivalent, since the converse is not necessarily true.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            For some scalars <m>c_1,\ldots, c_k</m>,
            <m>c_1\vvec_1+\cdots + c_k\vvec_k=\wvec</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Yes! This is the definition of span.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            The vector <m>\wvec</m> is a linear combination of the vectors in <m>S</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="no">
        <statement>
          <p>
            For any scalars <m>c_1,\ldots, c_k</m>,
            <m>c_1\vvec_1+\cdots + c_k\vvec=\wvec</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            The only way this statement could be true for all possible scalars
            is if all the vectors involved are zero.
            Otherwise, changing a scalar is going to change the resulting linear combination.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="no">
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\wvec = \vvec_i</m> for some <m>i=1,\ldots, k</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Although each vector in <m>S</m> belongs to the span of <m>S</m>,
            the span of <m>S</m> contains much more than just the vectors in <m>S</m>!
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

   <!-- Based on OPL problem Library/WHFreeman/Holt_linear_algebra/Chaps_1-4/2.2.63.pg -->
    <exercise xml:id="ex-ww-span4" label="ex-ww-span4">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>\uvec_4</m> be a vector that is not a linear combination of the vectors <m>\uvec_1,\uvec_2,\uvec_3</m>.
          Select the best statement.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="no">
          <statement>
            <p>
              We only know that the span of <m>\{\uvec_1,\uvec_2,\uvec_3\}</m> is a subset of the span of <m>\{\uvec_1,\uvec_2,\uvec_3,\uvec_4\}</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>
              The span of <m>\{\uvec_1,\uvec_2,\uvec_3\}</m> is a proper subset of the span of <m>\{\uvec_1,\uvec_2,\uvec_3,\uvec_4\}</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="no">
          <statement>
            <p>
              We only know that the span of <m>\{\uvec_1,\uvec_2,\uvec_3,\uvec_4\}</m> is a subset of the span of <m>\{\uvec_1,\uvec_2,\uvec_3\}</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="no">
          <statement>
            <p>
              There is no obvious relationship between the span of <m>\{\uvec_1,\uvec_2,\uvec_3\}</m>
              and the span of <m>\{\uvec_1,\uvec_2,\uvec_3,\uvec_4\}</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="no">
          <statement>
            <p>
              The span of <m>\{\uvec_1,\uvec_2,\uvec_3\}</m> is equal to the span of <m>\{\uvec_1,\uvec_2,\uvec_3,\uvec_4\}</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </choice>
      </choices>  
    </exercise>

    <!-- Based on the OPL problem at Library/WHFreeman/Holt_linear_algebra/Chaps_1-4/2.2.62.pg -->
    <exercise xml:id="ex-ww-span5" label="ex-ww-span5">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>\uvec_4</m> be a linear combination of the vectors <m>\uvec_1,\uvec_2,\uvec_3</m>.
          Select the best statement.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="no">
          <statement>
            <p>
              There is no obvious relationship between the span of <m>\{\uvec_1,\uvec_2,\uvec_3\}</m>
              and the span of <m>\{\uvec_1,\uvec_2,\uvec_3,\uvec_4\}</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="no">
          <statement>
            <p>
              We only know that the span of <m>\{\uvec_1,\uvec_2,\uvec_3\}</m> is a subset of the span of <m>\{\uvec_1,\uvec_2,\uvec_3,\uvec_4\}</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="no">
            <statement>
              <p>
                We only know that the span of <m>\{\uvec_1,\uvec_2,\uvec_3,\uvec_4\}</m> is a subset of the span of <m>\{\uvec_1,\uvec_2,\uvec_3\}</m>.
              </p>
            </statement>
          </choice>
          <choice correct="yes">
            <statement>
              <p>
                The span of <m>\{\uvec_1,\uvec_2,\uvec_3\}</m> is equal to the span of <m>\{\uvec_1,\uvec_2,\uvec_3,\uvec_4\}</m>.
              </p>
            </statement>
          </choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

</exercises>

</section>
